menu "ESP WebDAV Server"

    config ESP_WEBDAV_MAX_PATH_LEN
        int "Maximum resolved filesystem path length"
        default 600
        range 128 2048
        help
            Size of the stack buffer used to build the full filesystem path
            (root_path + decoded, sanitized URI) for every request. Requests
            that would resolve to a longer path are rejected with
            414 URI Too Long. Increase this if you use deeply nested
            directories or long file names.

    config ESP_WEBDAV_IO_BUF_SIZE
        int "File I/O chunk size"
        default 4096
        range 512 16384
        help
            Size of the buffer used to stream file contents for GET/PUT and
            for COPY. Larger values are faster but use more stack/heap per
            request.

    config ESP_WEBDAV_HTTPD_STACK_SIZE
        int "HTTP server task stack size"
        default 8192
        range 4096 32768
        help
            Stack size for the esp_http_server worker task that runs every
            WebDAV handler. Recursive operations (PROPFIND with
            "Depth: infinity", COPY/DELETE on a directory tree) put one
            ESP_WEBDAV_MAX_PATH_LEN-sized path buffer (or two, for COPY) on
            the stack per directory level, on top of esp_http_server's own
            usage -- the default esp_http_server stack size (4096) is not
            enough headroom for that plus more than one or two levels of
            nesting. Increase this further if you use a larger
            ESP_WEBDAV_MAX_PATH_LEN and deeply nested directories.

    config ESP_WEBDAV_DEBUG_CONNECTIONS
        bool "Log connecting peers and the first bytes they send"
        default n
        help
            Logs the address of every accepted connection, plus a hex dump
            of the first chunk of data received on it (later reads on the
            same connection are not dumped, so uploads stay readable).

            Meant for diagnosing clients that aren't speaking HTTP, which
            esp_http_server otherwise reports only as
            "parse_block: incomplete (0/N) with parser error = 16" with no
            indication of who sent what. A leading 0x16 0x03 is a TLS
            handshake (someone using https:// against the plain-HTTP port),
            "PRI * HTTP/2.0" is an HTTP/2 prior-knowledge preface.

            Writes request data to the log, including any Authorization
            header the client sends (Mountain Duck sends Basic credentials
            even when the server asks for none), so leave this off in
            production and scrub captured logs before sharing them.

    config ESP_WEBDAV_BODY_TIMEOUT_S
        int "Request body receive timeout (seconds)"
        default 30
        range 5 600
        help
            How long to keep waiting for more request-body data from a
            client that has stopped sending (a PUT upload, or the drained
            body of a PROPFIND/PROPPATCH/LOCK) before giving up with
            408 Request Timeout and closing the connection.

            esp_http_server runs every handler in a single task, so a
            handler that waits forever blocks the whole server, not just
            its own connection. This bound is what stops a client that
            disappears mid-upload (sleeping laptop, dropped WiFi) from
            wedging the server until the device is rebooted.

    config ESP_WEBDAV_ALLOW_DEPTH_INFINITY
        bool "Allow 'Depth: infinity' on PROPFIND"
        default y
        help
            RFC 4918 lets a PROPFIND request recursively list an entire
            subtree with a single "Depth: infinity" header. On a
            flash-backed filesystem with many files this can take a while
            and hold the response chunked-transfer open for a long time.
            Disabling this makes the server treat "Depth: infinity" the
            same as "Depth: 1" (children only, no grandchildren).

endmenu
